gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| ## Where the project's rules are
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| 
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| Your system prompt carries **no project context**, so what `AGENTS.md` would have handed you is here once:
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| 
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| - `AGENTS.md`, in the project root, is the project's **document map**: it routes a kind of change to the
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|   `guidelines/` document that owns the rule. Find your change's row there, then read the section it names — never the
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|   whole file.
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| - A guideline's section list is its own: `tools/look.sh '^## ' --path guidelines/<file>.md` prints it.
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| - The shipped capabilities are under `openspec/specs/`, one `spec.md` per capability; the change you are gating is in
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|   the change root, and the evidence it cites is versioned in that change's `evidence/`.
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| 
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| **A read that arrived cut is not the file.** A tool result can be a window: `tools/pi-trim-result.mjs` cuts every
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| result above `LOOP_TOOL_RESULT_MAX_CHARS` (`tools/loop/loop.conf`) and appends `[LOOP-TRIMMED: … the first N are shown
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| — read the rest in ranges (ctx_read mode="lines:N-M", or sed -n 'N,Mp')]`, and the read tool stops at its own cap
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| (about 2 000 lines or 50 kB per result, `docs/2026-11-07-reviewer-model-analysis.md`) — so a result that
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| carries that marker, or that ends before the file's last line, is the file's head and not the file. Page it with
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| `ctx_read`'s `offset` and `limit` until the last line arrives, and quote, cite or edit only a window that arrived whole
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| (issue #818).
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| 
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| ## What your own shell and tools refuse
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| 
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| None of the four families below is a question you can ask and none of them is a rule of this project to be argued
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| with: each is what the process you are started in refuses, before any rule of this tree can matter (issue #799).
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| 
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| - **You commit nothing and stage nothing.** The run commits, and the branch the item descends from is the base a
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|   red-on-HEAD proof is taken against, so `git commit` and `git add` are no step of yours — and `git stash`,
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|   `git checkout`, `git apply` and `git worktree` come back refused, because the approval they need is one an
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|   unattended process can never be asked for.
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| - **No inline code.** `python3 -c`, `python3 <<'EOF'` and a `$( … )` substitution are refused. A number is
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|   taken by a command the tree already carries — the project's measuring command, the suite a change names, a
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|   `tools/*.sh` — or by `awk` over the output such a command printed.
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| - **An edit needs the text as it stands now.** A body read twenty turns ago comes back `could not find the exact
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|   text`, or `found N occurrences` when the text is not unique: read the range once with `ctx_read` immediately before
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|   the edit, or edit with `ctx_patch`, whose hash refuses with a conflict instead of writing the wrong text.
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| - **A shell search of the tree is refused**: the verbs `grep`, `cat`, `head`, `tail`, `ls`, `find`, `wc` and `awk`
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|   over the tree are `tools/look.sh` (a pattern over the whole tree), `ctx_read` (one file, or a range of it) and
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|   `ctx_ls` (a directory). A range read (`sed -n 'N,Mp'`, `tail -n`) and a filter of another command's output are not
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|   refused.
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| 
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| ## Phase E — the findings, then the gate
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| 
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| 1. **Apply the findings, if there are any, and nothing else.** The reviewer's report is `{{FINDINGS_PATH}}`; when that
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|    value says the round found no blocking finding, there is nothing to apply and you go straight to step 2. Each
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|    `BLOCKING:` line is one thing to change, and the whole list is answered in **one pass**: a deletion, a corrected
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|    pointer, a narrower wording, the assertion the scenario names. A finding that would need a claim, a case or an
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|    assertion the change does not have is **not yours to invent** — apply what can be applied, and say what you left in
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|    the gate line's own words rather than widening the change. Do not re-plan, do not re-diagnose, and do not re-read the
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|    item's whole package for a finding that already says what to do.
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| 2. **Run the project's gate once**, exactly as the run's configuration states it:
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| 
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|    ```
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|    {{GATE_CMD}}
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|    ```
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| 
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|    It is evidence, not a thing to retry: it is run once per item and its result is quoted, not improved. A gate that
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|    does not complete inside the run's budget — the run ends a child that outlasts its configured wall-clock, and that
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|    includes every command you started — is not yours to report: the run records the item `blocked` with your log
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|    itself, and a second attempt is not yours to make. Two attempts at one gate is how a run spends its whole budget
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|    inside one child. Do not run a suite, a focused case or a mutation set to "check" first: the gate runs them. The
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|    gate counts its own runs: a full one beyond `LOOP_GATE_RUNS_MAX` in this child is refused with exit `3` and hands
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|    you the command the delta owes instead, and that refusal is not a red gate — it is the engine saying this child has
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|    spent its measurements.
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| 3. **Copy every test result you cite into `{{EVIDENCE_DIR}}`** at the moment it is produced. A change that cites a
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|    result and carries none is refused by the run, and a copy taken after the build tree is gone is not a copy.
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| 4. **A gate that did not pass is not yours to repair.** The tail child records the item `blocked` with the failing
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|    output you printed. Do not delete the change, do not unstage anything, and do not edit the tree after the gate: the
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|    next attempt reads it as this item's work.
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate| 5. **Do not archive the change, do not commit, do not push, and do not touch the issue tracker.** The run's script owns
gate#theGateCompletesTheChangeBeforeTheGate|    git, the labels and the closure, and the tail child owns the archive. Leave the tree as the gate left it.
